cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Chronotopia: analysis of time-course data for circadian biology"
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata from this file,
  and state which version you used — the interface and the numerical defaults
  change between versions, and the changelog records what changed and when.
type: software
# TODO before archiving: add ORCIDs, and add `doi:` once the repository is
# archived (Zenodo will mint one from a GitHub release). Add co-authors here in
# the order they should be cited.
authors:
  - given-names: Borja
    family-names: Ferrero-Bordera
    email: borja.ferrero@med.uni-muenchen.de
    affiliation: "Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München; John Innes Centre"
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX"
repository-code: "https://github.com/borfebor/chronotopia"
url: "https://borfebor.github.io/chronotopia/"
abstract: >-
  Chronotopia is a browser-based application for analysing circadian
  time-course data. It covers preprocessing (smoothing, normalisation,
  detrending, entrainment handling), period estimation by Lomb-Scargle,
  wavelet transform, FFT, autocorrelation and a cosinor sweep, rhythmicity
  testing via MetaCycle and permutation cosinor, extraction of around one
  hundred features per sample, plate-format analysis for 6 to 384-well
  layouts, and publication-ready figures and reports. It is designed to work
  on both short omics timecourses and long reporter recordings.
keywords:
  - circadian rhythms
  - chronobiology
  - time series analysis
  - period estimation
  - rhythmicity
  - bioluminescence
  - transcriptomics
# GPL-3.0-or-later is required rather than chosen: pyBOAT, which Chronotopia
# imports at module level for its wavelet analysis, is GPL-3.0-or-later, so the
# distributed combined work must be under compatible terms. rpy2 is
# GPL-2.0-or-later and upgrades cleanly to v3.
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
version: 0.8.0
date-released: "2026-08-25"
